      "type": "mcq",
      "sectionPart": "A",
      "calculatorAllowed": false,
      "question": "If $f(x) = \\int_{0}^{x} (t^2 + 1)\\,dt$, then $f'(2) =$",
      "options": [
        {"id": "A", "text": "3"},
        {"id": "B", "text": "5"},
        {"id": "C", "text": "$\\frac{8}{3}$"},
        {"id": "D", "text": "$2\\sqrt{5}$"}
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "B",
      "explanation": "By the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, $f'(x) = x^2 + 1$. So $f'(2) = 2^2 + 1 = 5$.",
      "topics": ["Fundamental Theorem of Calculus", "Derivatives"]
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "type": "mcq",
      "sectionPart": "A",
      "calculatorAllowed": false,
      "question": "The function $f$ is differentiable. If $f(1) = 3$ and $f'(x) = 2x + 1$ for all $x$, then $f(x) =$",
      "options": [
        {"id": "A", "text": "$x^2 + x + 3$"},
        {"id": "B", "text": "$x^2 + x + 1$"},
        {"id": "C", "text": "$x^2 + x$"},
        {"id": "D", "text": "$2x + 1$"}
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "B",
      "explanation": "Integrate: $f(x) = x^2 + x + C$. Since $f(1) = 1 + 1 + C = 3$, we get $C = 1$. So $f(x) = x^2 + x + 1$.",
      "topics": ["Integration", "Initial Value Problems"]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "type": "mcq",
      "sectionPart": "A",
      "calculatorAllowed": false,
      "question": "What is $\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\sin(3x)}{x}$?",
      "options": [
        {"id": "A", "text": "0"},
        {"id": "B", "text": "1"},
        {"id": "C", "text": "3"},
        {"id": "D", "text": "$\\infty$"}
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "C",
      "explanation": "$\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\sin(3x)}{x} = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{3\\cos(3x)}{1} = 3$ by L'Hôpital's Rule.",
      "topics": ["Limits", "L'Hôpital's Rule"]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "type": "mcq",
      "sectionPart": "A",
      "calculatorAllowed": false,
      "question": "If $y = \\ln(x^2 + 1)$, then $\\frac{dy}{dx} =$",
      "options": [
        {"id": "A", "text": "$\\frac{2x}{x^2 + 1}$"},
        {"id": "B", "text": "$\\frac{1}{x^2 + 1}$"},
        {"id": "C", "text": "$\\frac{2x}{x^2}$"},
        {"id": "D", "text": "$\\frac{x^2 + 1}{2x}$"}
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "A",
      "explanation": "Chain rule: $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{1}{x^2+1} \\cdot 2x = \\frac{2x}{x^2+1}$.",
      "topics": ["Chain Rule", "Derivatives of Logarithms"]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "type": "mcq",
      "sectionPart": "A",
      "calculatorAllowed": false,
      "question": "The series $\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{x^n}{n!}$ converges for",
      "options": [
        {"id": "A", "text": "only $x = 0$"},
        {"id": "B", "text": "$|x| < 1$"},
        {"id": "C", "text": "$|x| < e$"},
        {"id": "D", "text": "all real $x$"}
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "D",
      "explanation": "This is the Taylor series for $e^x$, which converges for all real $x$. The ratio test gives $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\frac{|x|}{n+1} = 0$ for all $x$.",
      "topics": ["Power Series", "Taylor Series"]
